Yes, this is a critical state. Having 13 to 14 GB of dirty bytes, 25.9 million evicted pages, and 99% swap memory usage on a node with 136 GB RAM means the secondary node is experiencing severe memory pressure and disk thrashing.

Dirty Bytes Exceed Safe Thresholds:
On a 136 GB RAM system, WiredTiger’s cache defaults to ~67.5 GB (50% RAM - 1 GB).

WiredTiger's eviction_dirty_trigger is 20%.

20% of 67.5 GB is ~13.5 GB.

Because dirty bytes hit ~14 GB, WiredTiger has crossed its emergency limit. It has stopped relying solely on background eviction threads and is forcing application and replication threads to flush pages to disk.

25.9 Million Evicted Pages:
This metric confirms that worker and application threads are constantly kicking pages out of RAM to keep the engine from crashing. Because writing dirty pages to disk is too slow, memory fills up immediately again.

99% Swap Usage (The Performance Killer):
When Linux starts swapping out MongoDB pages to disk:

Memory access times jump from nanoseconds (RAM) to milliseconds (Disk).

Lock wait times (parallelBatchWriterMode, global:w) skyrocket because threads are blocked waiting on disk reads/writes from swap.

This is what triggers Flow Control on the primary and degrades overall cluster throughput.


Immediate Action Plan
Step 1: Clamp WiredTiger Cache Size Down (To Prevent OS Swapping)
When OS swap hits 99%, it means non-WiredTiger processes (or MongoDB in-memory allocations like aggregation buffers, connections, and index builds) are competing with the cache for system RAM.

Drop the cache size slightly so the OS has breathing room and stops swapping. Run on the secondary:

// Temporarily reduce WT Cache from 67GB to 50GB to free OS RAM and kill swap pressure
db.adminCommand({
  "setParameter": 1,
  "wiredTigerEngineRuntimeConfig": "cache_size=50G"
});

Step 2: Clear/Turn Off Linux Swappiness
MongoDB requires OS swappiness to be set to 0 or 1. If it's set to the default (60), Linux will prematurely dump memory to disk.

Check current swappiness: cat /proc/sys/vm/swappiness

Set swappiness to 1 dynamically (as root/sudo on the OS):

Bash
sysctl vm.swappiness=1

Step 3: Increase Eviction Threads
With 56 CPU cores, you have plenty of compute available. You can scale WiredTiger background eviction threads so dedicated workers handle page flushes rather than blocking query threads:

JavaScript
db.adminCommand({
  "setParameter": 1,
  "wiredTigerEngineRuntimeConfig": "eviction=(threads_min=8,threads_max=16)"
});
Step 4: Verify Disk IOPS Bottleneck
Check storage utilization on the secondary host using OS tools:

Bash
iostat -xz 1

