How to Resolve the Bottleneck
Upgrade Secondary Disk Performance: Ensure the secondary node’s storage provisioned IOPS and write bandwidth match or exceed the primary’s disk specs.

Tune WiredTiger Eviction Workers (If CPU Available): Increase the number of background eviction threads in mongod.conf or dynamically via setParameter:

JavaScript
db.adminCommand({ setParameter: 1, wiredTigerEngineRuntimeConfig: "eviction=(threads_min=4,threads_max=8)" })
Tune Oplog Application Batch Sizes: If you are running massive bulk writes, lower the maximum batch size applied by replication workers on secondaries:

JavaScript
db.adminCommand({ setParameter: 1, replBatchLimitBytes: 16777216 }) // 16MB limit
Temporary Mitigation for Primary Throughput: If Flow Control is stalling critical application writes, you can adjust target lag thresholds or temporarily disable Flow Control (though this will increase secondary replication lag):

JavaScript
db.adminCommand({ setParameter: 1, enableFlowControl: false })
(Note: Disabling flow control allows primary writes to proceed at full speed, but the secondary will fall further behind until storage I/O is resolved).

Is This Related to Flow Control Increasing?
Yes, absolutely. The two metrics are directly linked in a primary-secondary feedback loop:

WiredTiger Eviction Bottleneck: When dirty bytes cross threshold limits (typically around 20% of WiredTiger cache), background eviction threads stop being able to write pages to disk fast enough. WiredTiger forces application/replication worker threads to assist with page eviction, dramatically slowing down write application.

Oplog Application Lags: Because the secondary node’s WiredTiger cache is choked with dirty data, it cannot apply incoming oplog entries from the primary in a timely manner.

Flow Control Kicks In: To prevent the secondary from falling so far behind that it drops out of the oplog window, MongoDB's Flow Control algorithm throttles write throughput on the primary. As secondary replication lag builds, Flow Control steadily increases its pressure, putting backpressure on client writes.

What Causes Dirty Bytes to Rise to 13–14 GB?
I/O Subsystem Exhaustion: The underlying disk volume (EBS, SAN, local NVMe) has hit its IOPS or throughput limit. The disk simply cannot write pages fast enough to clear the dirty page queue.

Aggressive Parallel Replication: The secondary is receiving bulk write batches via parallelBatchWriterMode and pushing them into WiredTiger faster than the disk can flush checkpoints.

Under-provisioned Secondary: The secondary node has slower hardware, fewer CPU cores, or lower IOPS allocation compared to the primary node.

Checkpoints Stalling: WiredTiger takes a checkpoint every 60 seconds (or 2 GB of log data). If flushing dirty pages during a checkpoint takes too long, dirty data rapidly accumulates.
